1:["$","div",null,{"className":"page","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"page-title","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"eyebrow","children":"The paper"}],["$","h1",null,{"children":"A negative solution to the complemented subspace problem for Banach spaces with unconditional bases"}],["$","p",null,{"children":["Antonio Acuaviva"," · ","5 September 2026"]}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"paper-layout","children":[["$","section",null,{"children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"Abstract"}],["$","$Le",null,{"text":"We give a negative solution to the complemented subspace problem for Banach spaces with unconditional bases over both the real and complex fields. For every $\\rho>0$, we construct a projection $P_\\rho$ of norm less than $1+\\rho$ on a separable superreflexive space\n\\[\nX_\\rho=\\left(\\bigoplus_{j=1}^{\\infty}\\ell_{p_j}^{N_j}\\right)_2,\\qquad p_j\\downarrow2,\n\\]\nsuch that $Z_\\rho=P_\\rho(X_\\rho)$ and its dual $Z_\\rho^*$ have Schauder bases but admit no unconditional bases. Over the real field, both spaces have Gordon–Lewis local unconditional structure (GL-lust) but fail Dubinsky–Pełczyński–Rosenthal local unconditional structure (DPR-lust), disproving a conjecture of Figiel, Johnson and Tzafriri. In particular, neither is isomorphic to a Banach lattice, giving a negative solution to the separable Banach-lattice complemented subspace problem. A modification of the construction also shows that the class of separable real Banach lattices is not primary. A Lean 4 formalisation of the main results accompanies the paper."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"note","style":{"marginTop":20},"children":"The abstract describes further results beyond the five Lean theorems presented on this site."}]]}],["$","aside",null,{"className":"paper-meta","children":[["$","p",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Manuscript"}],26," pages",["$","br",null,{}],"5 September 2026"]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"publication-links","children":[["$","a",null,{"className":"arxiv-badge","href":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.08921","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer","referrerPolicy":"no-referrer","aria-label":"Read the paper on arXiv","children":["$","img",null,{"src":"/images/arxiv-logo.jpg","alt":"arXiv","width":800,"height":504}]}],[]]}],["$","p",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Start with"}],["$","a",null,{"href":"/paper/manuscript.pdf#page=2","children":"Theorems A, B & Corollary C · pp. 2–3"}]]}]]}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"links","children":[["$","a",null,{"className":"link-button primary","href":"/paper/manuscript.pdf","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener","children":"View PDF ↗"}],["$","a",null,{"className":"link-button","href":"/paper/manuscript.pdf","download":"The_complemented_subspace_problem.pdf","children":"Download PDF"}],["$","a",null,{"className":"link-button","href":"/","children":"Explore the formalised results →"}]]}],["$","iframe",null,{"className":"pdf-viewer","src":"/paper/manuscript.pdf#view=FitH","title":"The paper PDF, 26 pages"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"note","style":{"marginTop":15},"children":"If your browser does not display the embedded PDF, use “View PDF” or “Download PDF” above."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"hash","children":["PDF SHA256: ","a78ef57936a5f9b47f5da923703a17c1e280cd397335dfcf9a55fa21b5a31cb9"]}]]}]
